            <ArticleTitle>Effectiveness of kampo medicine (sai-boku-to) in treatment of patients with glossodynia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>682-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to evaluate the usefulness of a Kampo medicine (Sai-boku-to) for treatment of patients with glossodynia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sai-boku-to or an antianxiety drug (diazepam) with vitamin B complex was administered orally for 3 months to each of 200 patients with glossodynia. Clinical examination evaluated the following subjective symptoms: pain, burning sensation, and discomfort. Effectiveness was evaluated as follows: &quot;markedly effective,&quot; all 3 symptoms disappeared; &quot;effective,&quot; pain improved; &quot;ineffective,&quot; no improvement in pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The effective rates were 70% after 1 month, 85% after 2 months, and 92% after 3 months of administration of Sai-boku-to (the Kampo group) and 74% after 1 month, 71% after 2 months, and 69% after 3 months of administration of the antianxiety drug with vitamin B complex (the control group). No significant side effect was noted in the Kampo group, but sleepiness was recorded in 33 cases in the control group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results indicate that Sai-boku-to may be a clinically useful medicine for the long-term treatment of patients with glossodynia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
